WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.050
Run one small change with grok in spec with code. This

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:07.570
walkthrough prepares the task. It does not submit a provider

00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:07.870
run.

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:12.830
In Settings, Providers, choose Native Grok for an existing

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:17.770
CLI or Grok Build for a managed registry install. These are

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:21.870
distinct routes. Finish the selected agent's own sign -in and

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:23.130
choose an available model.

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:27.410
Open an example repository as a project. The main

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:31.390
conversation is the project coordinator. Check which machine

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:32.270
is doing the work.

00:00:33.017 --> 00:00:36.717
Select Grok, an available model, and the approval mode you

00:00:36.717 --> 00:00:40.637
intend to use. This tutorial does not require full access.

00:00:41.517 --> 00:00:45.917
Give it one bounded task. Add a named greeting, keep the no

00:00:45.917 --> 00:00:50.257
argument greeting working, and add tests for both. Ask it to

00:00:50.257 --> 00:00:52.177
describe the change before editing.

00:00:52.983 --> 00:00:57.403
Read the proposed change, then ask it to implement. Review

00:00:57.403 --> 00:01:00.443
any command approval rather than accepting it automatically.

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:05.610
Inspect the changed files and the actual test output. If a

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:09.590
check failed or was not run, keep that in the result. A

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:11.990
confident reply is not test evidence.

00:01:12.917 --> 00:01:16.877
Finish with the change, its diff, and actual test evidence.

00:01:17.317 --> 00:01:20.997
Use SpecWeave increments when you need a durable plan and

00:01:20.997 --> 00:01:24.097
task record. They are optional for this first task.
